<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Lakz</h1><p class="st-meta">700 CE – 1100 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A medieval Lezgian principality in southern Dagestan, known mainly from Arab geographers, that guarded the mountain hinterland of Derbent.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Lakz is known chiefly from Arab writers of the 10th century such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-masudi">al-Masudi</a>, who describe a numerous and warlike people in the mountains west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbent">Derbent</a>, generally identified with the ancestors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lezgins">Lezgins</a>. When and how it arose is not recorded; it appears as a principality by the 8th or 9th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-masudi">Al-Masudi</a> reports that the people of Lakz lived in fortified villages, some still pagan, some Muslim, under their own rulers. The land lay in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samur-river">Samur</a> valley and neighbouring mountains, with farming and herding.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 11th century Lakz was drawn into the wars of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvanshahs</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-derbent">Emirate of Derbent</a> and then overrun by Seljuk raids. It disappears from the sources, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lezgins">Lezgin</a> communities of its valleys remained.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 700 CE – 1100 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Caucasus</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:73.63%;width:7.96%"></span></div><div 